  completeExplanation: 'The chapter presents a comprehensive, hands-on progression from empirical shape observation to deductive geometric reasoning. Students begin by recognizing that complex real-world structures decompose into standard geometric solids. Unfolding a 3D box introduces the concept of a net. Students categorize polyhedra into prisms (which feature two identical parallel bases joined by rectangular lateral faces) and pyramids (which feature a single polygon base and triangular lateral faces converging to an apex). By counting physical components, students discover Euler\'s polyhedron characteristic: F + V - E = 2. The unit then transitions to angle concepts: an angle is formed when two rays meet at a vertex. Using a square-corner folded paper tool, students benchmark angles as right, acute (< 90 deg), or obtuse (> 90 deg), and discover why triangles provide structural rigidity while flexible rectangles distort into parallelograms. In circle geometry, students define a circle as the locus of points equidistant from a centre, proving that all radii are equal and establishing the fundamental formula Diameter = 2 * Radius. The chapter culminates in spatial puzzle-solving, dissection challenges, and Euler-path traversals.',
